      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(google): cancel unread response body on video download error",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nWhen `downloadGeneratedVideoFromUri` receives a non-OK response (e.g. 503), it throws immediately without canceling the response body. The unconsumed stream holds a connection open.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nFollows the same pattern as #109701, #109519, #109728, #110039 — cancel the response body before throwing.\n\n## User Impact\n\nNo user-facing change. Prevents resource leaks when Google video generation downloads fail with non-OK HTTP status codes.\n\n## Evidence\n\n### Real HTTP server proof\n\n```\n$ node scripts/proofs/google-video-cancel-proof.mjs\nServer: http://127.0.0.1:38965\n\n=== BEFORE (throw without body.cancel) ===\n  503 Service Unavailable — body UNCONSUMED, throw immediately\n  [server] /generated-video:download?alt=media&key=test → stream completed (no cancel)\n\n=== AFTER (body?.cancel before throw) ===\n  503 Service Unavailable\n  body?.cancel() executed ✓\n  [server] /generated-video:download?alt=media&key=test → client canceled ✓\n\n=== Error message preserved ===\n  Thrown: Failed to download Google generated video: 503 Service Unavailable\n```\n\nThe proof script simulates a Google direct-URI download endpoint returning 503 with a slow binary stream, then exercises the cancel-before-throw pattern.\n\n### Unit test\n\nNew test (\"cancels response body on non-OK direct URI download before throwing\") verifies `body.cancel()` is called once before the error propagates, using `ReadableStream({ cancel })` spy pattern.",
